00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.230
Leah: What is Agile development?

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.960
Chris: Let’s have a look
at this in more detail.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.950
Leah: ‘Agile Development’ which is
also more commonly known as Agile

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.569
Software Development, is an umbrella
term for a range of frameworks,

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:18.119
such as ‘Scrum’, and practices such
as the ‘daily stand-up meeting’.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:22.259
It adopts the principles set
out in the ‘Agile Manifesto’

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.520
and the ‘Agile 12 Principles’.

00:00:24.819 --> 00:00:27.171
Chris: The Agile Manifesto is a
short document, which was created

00:00:27.171 --> 00:00:31.191
in 2001 from a group of leading
professionals who met to discuss

00:00:31.191 --> 00:00:32.991
the future of software development.

00:00:33.541 --> 00:00:39.051
Its focus is on value to individuals and
interactions, working software, customer

00:00:39.051 --> 00:00:41.411
collaboration and responding to change.

00:00:41.891 --> 00:00:46.591
The Manifesto itself is short and
made up of 68 words and has since

00:00:46.601 --> 00:00:51.451
been embraced by many different teams
and companies, and it is agreed by

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:53.441
many that it is still relevant today.

00:00:54.591 --> 00:00:59.471
Leah: The 12 Principles of Agile Software,
expand on the values within the Manifesto.

00:01:00.181 --> 00:01:04.031
The focus is on the customer,
continuous delivery of product,

00:01:04.201 --> 00:01:10.831
change, frequency, team collaboration,
motivation, support, communication,

00:01:11.081 --> 00:01:16.431
measurement, excellence, simplicity,
self-organisation, and reflection.

00:01:17.371 --> 00:01:21.951
Agile came about as a process to
address issues that software developers

00:01:21.961 --> 00:01:27.151
faced, focussing on a project being
flexible, so it could respond to any

00:01:27.161 --> 00:01:29.801
changes and deal with any uncertainty.

00:01:30.341 --> 00:01:34.611
Chris: Agile is people-oriented and
result-focused in respect to software

00:01:34.651 --> 00:01:38.531
delivery and instead of delivering all
the product at the end of the project,

00:01:38.771 --> 00:01:43.081
the software is built incrementally
from the start in time-boxed 'sprints'.

00:01:44.331 --> 00:01:48.461
It is an iterative approach used in
software development projects with a

00:01:48.461 --> 00:01:53.451
focus on continuous releases that takes
customer feedback into consideration.

00:01:54.291 --> 00:01:59.361
Development tasks are broken down into
bite size chunks, which can be achieved

00:01:59.371 --> 00:02:01.901
in a certain period known as a ‘sprint’.

00:02:02.551 --> 00:02:06.111
Each task would be assigned a
measurement of time or complexity.

00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:11.151
The self-organised agile team itself would
normally consist of different skillsets,

00:02:11.661 --> 00:02:17.331
working independently and collaboratively,
to achieve the tasks within that set time.

00:02:18.191 --> 00:02:22.161
Leah: Agile development’s iterative
approach means that continuous testing

00:02:22.191 --> 00:02:26.641
and continuous improvement takes place
throughout the entire product lifecycle

00:02:27.181 --> 00:02:31.971
this gives early warnings of quality
issues, reduces the risk of failure,

00:02:32.361 --> 00:02:37.041
and gives opportunities to find new ways
to fix those issues and or processes.

00:02:37.561 --> 00:02:41.571
There is an emphasis on frequent
continuous releases with customer

00:02:41.701 --> 00:02:44.781
transparency and visibility
on the work in progress.

00:02:46.451 --> 00:02:49.411
Chris: Project planning is also
ongoing throughout the project

00:02:49.491 --> 00:02:53.921
which is known as just-in-time
[also a method used in ‘LEAN’].

00:02:54.491 --> 00:02:58.001
Estimates for each stage or
‘Sprint’ are relative and these are

00:02:58.001 --> 00:03:00.081
refined as the project progresses.

00:03:00.641 --> 00:03:04.311
Features also begin more general
and details are added later.

00:03:04.991 --> 00:03:08.261
As not all the details are available
at the beginning of the project,

00:03:08.571 --> 00:03:12.181
features being added later are
often influenced by other members

00:03:12.221 --> 00:03:13.871
or the team and the customer.

